<CompareLayout
  title="Noughtpad vs Bear: two quiet Markdown note apps"
  h1="Noughtpad vs Bear"
  other="Bear"
  description="Noughtpad vs Bear compared honestly: both are calm Markdown writing apps. Bear is polished and Apple-only with iCloud sync; Noughtpad keeps plain files on Mac, Windows and Linux and finds connections for you."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Bear and Noughtpad share a taste for quiet writing with Markdown. Bear is a polished app for Apple devices that keeps your notes in its own library. Noughtpad keeps every note as a plain file and focuses on helping you see how your notes connect.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choose Bear</strong> if you only use Apple devices, want your notes on your iPhone and iPad too, and care most about a beautiful, mature writing app.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose Noughtpad</strong> if you want your notes as plain files you can open anywhere, use Windows or Linux too, or want related notes, ideas over time and chat with your notes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Bear does better</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>iPhone, iPad and Mac</strong>, synced through iCloud with Bear Pro. Noughtpad has no phone app yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Polish.</strong> Bear has had years of careful design work, with themes and a refined editor.</li>
    <li><strong>Export formats.</strong> Markdown for free; HTML, Word, PDF, images and ePub with Pro.</li>
    <li><strong>Note encryption</strong> with Pro.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Noughtpad does differently</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plain files, always.</strong> Your notes are Markdown files in a folder from the start, not something you export. Edit them in any app.</li>
    <li><strong>Mac, Windows and Linux.</strong> Bear is for Apple devices only.</li>
    <li><strong>Connections and ideas over time.</strong> <a href="/guide/related-notes/">Related notes</a>, search by meaning, <a href="/guide/ideas-over-time/">ideas over time</a> and chat with your notes, all running on your computer by default.</li>
    <li><strong>Free.</strong> Noughtpad has no paid tier today.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Side by side</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Noughtpad</th><th scope="col">Bear</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Price</th><td>Free</td><td>Free; Bear Pro $2.99 a month or $29.99 a year</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Devices</th><td>Mac (Apple Silicon), Windows, Linux</td><td>Mac, iPhone, iPad</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Sync</th><td>Google Drive sync in testing, or any folder sync</td><td>iCloud, with Bear Pro</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Where notes live</th><td>Markdown files in your folder</td><td>Bear's own library; export to Markdown and more</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Related notes and ideas over time</th><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Chat with your notes</th><td>Yes, local model or your own provider</td><td>No</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Moving from Bear</h2>
  <p>
    Export your notes from Bear as Markdown, and put the files in your Noughtpad folder (or choose that folder in Noughtpad). Noughtpad reads Markdown files with or without a header.
  </p>

